<header><p class="enc-kicker">People / group</p><h1 class="enc-title">Ma'n dynasty</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Ma'nids, Maan dynasty, Banu Ma'n</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Ma'n dynasty were <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/druze">Druze</a> chiefs of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chouf">Chouf</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mount-lebanon">Mount Lebanon</a> who rose under Ottoman rule in the 16th century. Their most famous member, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fakhr-al-din-ii">Fakhr al-Din II</a>, built a large autonomous domain over much of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lebanon">Lebanon</a> and northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/palestine">Palestine</a> before the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottomans</a> executed him in 1635. When the line died out in 1697, leadership of Mount Lebanon passed to their relatives the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shihab-dynasty">Shihabs</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/man-dynasty.jpg" alt="Ma'n dynasty" width="320" height="320" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Original: Eugene Ipavec Vector: Germenfer · CC0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Maanid_Emirate_Flag.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>Traditional Lebanese histories bring the family to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chouf">Chouf</a> in the 12th century, where its founder, said to come from a clan of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rabia">Rabi'a</a> confederation, fought the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/crusader-states">Crusader</a> lords of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/beirut">Beirut</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sidon">Sidon</a> beside the Tanukhid Buhturids; the historian Kamal Salibi judged these accounts without foundation. They may have belonged to a wider settlement of Arab tribesmen in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mount-lebanon">Mount Lebanon</a> by the rulers of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/damascus">Damascus</a> as a buffer against the Crusader coast. The first member whose existence is certain is Fakhr al-Din I, who died in 1506, was called emir of the Chouf, and founded the Fakhreddine Mosque at the family seat of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/deir-al-qamar">Deir al-Qamar</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>In 1518 three Ma'nid chiefs were arrested by Janbirdi al-Ghazali, governor of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/damascus">Damascus</a>, and released by Sultan <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/selim-i">Selim I</a> after a heavy fine. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/druze">Druze</a> of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chouf">Chouf</a> were repeatedly attacked by Ottoman expeditions for evading tax collectors and hoarding firearms, and after the destructive campaign of 1585 the emir Qurqumaz ibn Yunis died in hiding in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/keserwan">Kisrawan</a>.</p><p>His son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fakhr-al-din-ii">Fakhr al-Din II</a> worked with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottomans</a> instead, becoming governor of Sidon-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/beirut">Beirut</a> in 1593 and of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/safed">Safed</a> in 1602. After a campaign against him in 1613 he fled to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a>, returned in 1618, defeated Yusuf Sayfa of Tripoli and extended his rule into Maronite northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mount-lebanon">Mount Lebanon</a>, but a second Ottoman expedition in 1633 killed most of the family.</p><p>His nephew Mulhim, son of Yunus, drove out the rival <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/druze">Druze</a> chief Ali Alam al-Din and in 1642 became tax farmer of the Druze Mountain. Mulhim's son Qurqumaz was killed by the governor of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/damascus">Damascus</a> in 1662, according to Istifan al-Duwayhi, while his brother Ahmad defeated the Alam al-Dins in 1667 and held the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chouf">Chouf</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/keserwan">Kisrawan</a> despite campaigns against him for backing Shia rebels.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Legacy</h2><p>After Ahmad died without a son, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/druze">Druze</a> chiefs chose Bashir Shihab, whose mother was Mulhim's daughter, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shihab-dynasty">Shihabs</a> held the region until the expulsion of Bashir II in 1841. Historians see the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emirate">emirate</a> of the Ma'ns and Shihabs as an early precursor of present-day <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lebanon">Lebanon</a>. In <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tyre">Tyre</a>, the ruins of the family's residence, later a khan, stand in the souk; Israeli bombing in 1982 partly destroyed them.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Ma'n dynasty” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Their politics turned on rivalry and alliance with the Druze <b>Ma'nids</b> and later the Shihabis of the Mount Lebanon Emirate.</blockquote><a href="/state/harfush-emirate">Harfush emirate</a></li><li><blockquote>The <b>Ma'n</b> family rose to dominate them, and under Fakhr al-Din II (1591-1633) their emirate extended over much of Lebanon, Galilee and the coast.</blockquote><a href="/state/mount-lebanon-emirate">Mount Lebanon Emirate</a></li></ul></section>
